WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:05.850
Now let's move on to the components and the architecture of the system.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.020
The very first thing to say is that we use the GitHub platform. It is

3
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:19.980
known to all software developers, because it is a rather old

4
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:26.120
platform. It is very friendly to open source. It

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:30.240
gives open-source

6
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:37.860
projects every means to grow, including computing

7
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.540
power, which it gives for free.

8
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:46.100
It is quite a mature platform with many tools, and we will go through all

9
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:52.980
the details and points: which components of this platform we use. So

10
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.700
to work with this platform we need to sign up.

11
00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.950
How do we sign up?

12
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.090
We need to go to github.com,

13
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.720
choose "Sign up" if you don't have an account, and, for example, sign up with a

14
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:20.660
Google account. Choose the Google account you already have. There you will only need

15
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.860
to give a nickname. Let's try it; I think it's like this. Let's confirm that

16
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:32.440
we can continue. And here all you will need is to enter a username,

17
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.940
pick one that is not taken yet.

18
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.040
Click "Continue", choose the country.

19
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.080
Of course, I don't know why it's here...

20
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.180
"Iran" is some kind of gesture on GitHub's part, I suppose.

21
00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:52.550
"Ukraine", and after that you don't need to tick the box.

22
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.650
They would just send you emails.

23
00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.250
And we press the green button and get our account.

24
00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:06.190
So our nickname will be, for example, this one, startlink, and our email.

25
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.030
Once we have signed up

26
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.300
and have this account, just send me that email or that

27
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:23.000
username, to be added to the team that works on the projects.

28
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.780
GitHub; let's go back to the plan.

29
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.880
What do we have in this GitHub?

30
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.880
There is a lot of code in GitHub.

31
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:40.590
Code here describes, of course, all the automated pipelines and the like.

32
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.710
The talks are also stored as code,

33
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:54.330
their transcripts, subtitles for each of the videos, and so on: metadata.

34
00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:00.570
We also have an important component in this repository that can be

35
00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:07.020
used no matter whether we work through this

36
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.260
portal or not, and whichever AI we use.

37
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.040
It is the glossary, a little dictionary,

38
00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.300
built from the Mantra Books,

39
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:26.120
from existing translations (there should be a description down here), from

40
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.120
the English Mantra Book for terminology, the Ukrainian Mantra Book for spelling,

41
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.400
Ukrainian spelling, in fact.

42
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:41.700
From other talks that had already been translated before

43
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:48.820
from English into Ukrainian, so as to build this

44
00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:54.560
dictionary. It has a set format, and it can be used

45
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.560
with any AI.

46
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:06.100
What does it give you? Simply the spelling: if it's

47
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.280
Kundalini, then it's Kundalini, like this.

48
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:19.240
So that the terminology in our translations agrees with

49
00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:25.400
how it is written, for example, in the Mantra Books, because the Mantra Books were not made

50
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.040
just like that, but on a sound basis.

51
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.530
So this artifact can be used on its own.

52
00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.410
There are even releases here.

53
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.400
There is an archive you can give, for example, to your AI:

54
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:48.250
"Take the archive, this release No. 3, sy-tools,

55
00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:54.530
sy-subtitles, and use it when translating

56
00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.990
my material locally."

57
00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:02.010
Does your AI support fetching files from the internet?

58
00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:08.960
If not, you can download the ZIP archive and give it to it, if it can only read

59
00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.740
local files. That is all about the dictionary for better

60
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:16.920
automatic translation.

61
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.480
The next component is the automation of translation and subtitle creation.

62
00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:32.190
I just want to explain how it works, for general understanding: there is

63
00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:38.110
another component, here, Actions, which

64
00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:44.100
has many different automated things. The ones that run by default

65
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.480
we did not make; they just run automatically.

66
00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.040
Various scanners from GitHub to improve the code,

67
00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.700
to find vulnerabilities and so on.

68
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.300
But our own ones: here, for example, one of our main pipelines in

69
00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.700
the automation is the "subtitle pipeline".

70
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:16.780
It... let's open it on the example of some already processed

71
00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:17.780
talk,

72
00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.290
one that needed it recently.

73
00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:26.850
Here, you see, is the title as it is on Amruta, and then

74
00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:32.360
which processes were run: Translate, Review and Build.

75
00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.400
The subtitle build, I mean.

76
00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.200
Here we can see...

77
00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.350
Let me think how to make it easier to see.

78
00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.620
Translate & Review has several steps.

79
00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:56.500
The only thing is, it doesn't show me details because I'm not signed in.

80
00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.780
You have to sign in with at least some account to see the details.

81
00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.580
Until now I was completely anonymous, so to speak.

82
00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.140
So, with my account...

83
00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:19.700
we sign in, and now we can see a little

84
00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:20.940
more detail.

85
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.420
What steps do we have here?

86
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.600
First we have the translation steps.

87
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:39.700
That is, there are instructions for the agent saying: you must work on

88
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:46.200
translating this particular talk, here is the metadata; so the agent must understand what it is working on,

89
00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.480
and later, if anything is interesting, I'll show it; it's all in the code.

90
00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:57.960
What instructions are passed to it, and can we even see that here?

91
00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.850
There! You see, it says here what it needs to read,

92
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:12.020
which files to load, which rules apply to it, and so on,

93
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.500
and it works. It translates.

94
00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:23.610
After it translates, we have a small step that checks

95
00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:30.150
whether the AI has messed something up, whether there are at least as many paragraphs.

96
00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:38.380
If it slipped so badly that the number of paragraphs differs, the run fails here.

97
00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.200
Next we have this review step,

98
00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.860
the automated 2 plus 1.

99
00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.480
What does that mean?

100
00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:54.600
It means that separate

101
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.560
agents are launched first.

102
00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.320
One agent is the linguist, so to speak.

103
00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:08.700
That is, the one who proofreads as a scholar of the Ukrainian language. It checks

104
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:15.680
for all kinds of spelling, punctuation, orthography,

105
00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.260
grammar and so on.

106
00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:24.480
And a separate reviewer is also a translator, one with knowledge of

107
00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.380
Sahaja Yoga, of the Sahaja Yoga domain; it also proofreads, so that if the first

108
00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:37.080
translator made some mistakes, it corrects them, because these artificial

109
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:42.420
intelligences, however many times you run them, may work a little

110
00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:43.420
differently each time.

111
00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.100
So if we put one on the big translation task, and the other not

112
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.800
on that but on finding specific flaws, it gives a better result.

113
00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:02.780
But there is also a third: these two run first in parallel, and the third is the Critic,

114
00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:08.580
a linguist who

115
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:14.800
understands the Sahaja Yoga domain through this dictionary or its general

116
00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.840
understanding, through the instructions.

117
00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:24.670
And it looks at what the first two found and filters out what may

118
00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:28.630
be out of place. So it also acts as an arbiter.

119
00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:35.370
Rather than simply: if one of these two reviewers glitched, roughly speaking,

120
00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.730
that becomes the final result.

121
00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:45.570
No! The output, the analysis results from those two go to the Critic.

122
00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:52.360
The Critic then analyses this limited amount of information to decide whether

123
00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.660
to apply these corrections or not.

124
00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:02.260
And maybe the two of them also conflict with each other in some places.

125
00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.800
It decides, and then it applies the changes.

126
00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:09.420
So this is the review phase, this 2 plus 1,

127
00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:12.940
and after that the changes are applied.

128
00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:20.580
Once they are applied, let's see, subtitles can also

129
00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:25.140
be created. These are also steps we can choose or not.

130
00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.740
In this case it was chosen, and it is here.

131
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:40.140
In general there is a procedure by which some intermediate material is first prepared,

132
00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:44.500
by which the Ukrainian sentences are split into pieces,

133
00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.880
which can be logical ones, at the full stops.

134
00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:56.360
Then one more agent is launched and finds, for these fragments into which

135
00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:01.180
the text was logically split, the matching timestamps,

136
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:07.340
and after that it assembles the subtitles.

137
00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:12.690
After that, there is the timecode format,

138
00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:17.620
a whole set of steps that lets us assemble the subtitles.

139
00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:21.900
After that an optimisation runs as well.

140
00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:29.010
This is exactly about our motivation to have subtitles

141
00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:35.690
of proper quality. That is, there is a whole list of steps that run

142
00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:40.310
to optimise our subtitles so they are comfortable to read.

143
00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:46.670
The main parameter is CPS, characters per

144
00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:53.260
second, so that subtitles are not shown

145
00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:59.600
too fast, because when the speech is fast, you can show during that fast

146
00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:03.760
speech our Ukrainian translation, but nobody will manage to read it,

147
00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:07.420
and it will all be uncomfortable.

148
00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:15.720
Yogis don't always watch only at home; it may be shown somewhere online

149
00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:21.360
or at a programme. And you can't pause or rewind,

150
00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.800
so this is an important point. And why else is it important?

151
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:33.840
Because Ukrainian, as we probably all know, is a little more

152
00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.180
character-heavy than English.

153
00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:43.340
And the same thing that can be read a bit faster in English needs a bit

154
00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:50.050
more time in Ukrainian. More characters per second have to be taken in, and so

155
00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:55.870
we arrive at the rule that we try to have no more than 15

156
00:13:55.870 --> 00:14:02.830
characters per second, but if the speech is very fast, then

157
00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:06.850
up to 20. That is our maximum.

158
00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:10.130
If we cannot achieve that,

159
00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:18.000
then this pipeline of ours will fail.

160
00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:22.420
It will say, "Sorry, we couldn't do it."

161
00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.900
And then you have to go and look by hand: why?

162
00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:31.980
What happened? Maybe the translator translated too wordily, or maybe there is some

163
00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:32.980
other problem.

164
00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:40.340
Overall the system keeps improving to cover different variants, different cases.

165
00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.040
Cases can be very different.

166
00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:47.320
Sometimes the video and the transcript don't match.

167
00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:48.900
For example,

168
00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:57.080
the video may lack part of the transcript. The transcript may be fuller,

169
00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.920
while in the video this fragment was lost.

170
00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.280
But someone recorded it from somewhere, the transcript.

171
00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:09.950
There are many such special cases, and if,

172
00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:15.910
while our pipeline runs, it turns out that something does not fit into the

173
00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:20.510
subtitles at all, or there are some problems, it turns red instead of green. We will see it and

174
00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:26.840
be able to handle the special cases separately. Also, as you know, we have

175
00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:30.000
several videos, usually.

176
00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:37.240
For example, there is the full Puja protocol and several talks. So we

177
00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:44.240
also build double subtitles. That is, we have the transcript we translate, and we

178
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:46.440
have two more separate subtitles,

179
00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.960
for two separate videos.
